<h2>The challenge</h2><p>Speech research is vulnerable to methodological pitfalls such as speaker overlap between training and test sets, small sample sizes, and misleading gains from overly complex representations.</p>
<h2>Technical approach</h2><ul><li>Build linguistic and acoustic representations and compare their predictive behavior.</li><li>Include interpretable baselines such as TF–IDF with a linear classifier.</li><li>Use speaker- or participant-grouped evaluation to reduce leakage across folds.</li><li>Compare individual representations and combinations while reporting suitable classification metrics and uncertainty.</li></ul>
<h2>The judgment behind the work</h2><p>A strong evaluation should separate the benefit of the representation from the benefit of model complexity. Careful grouped validation is critical when multiple examples can originate from the same person.</p>
<div class="callout">Why this project is here: it illustrates a combination of modeling judgment, engineering tradeoffs, and attention to the decisions the system supports—not a claim of performance without evidence.</div>
